---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { cycleRange, generatedAt } from "../lib/data";
---

<Base title="About the data" description="Where this data comes from, what's counted and how organizations are classified.">
  <p class="kicker">About the data</p>
  <h1>How this data works</h1>
  <p class="dek">
    Every number here comes from public filings: the FEC for money, SEC EDGAR and lobbying disclosures for who's
    behind each PAC. Here's what's counted, and what isn't.
  </p>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What's counted</h2>
    <p>
      Direct contributions from political committees (PACs, leadership PACs, party and candidate committees)
      to the campaigns of current members of Congress, {cycleRange}, as reported to the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.fec.gov/data/browse-data/?tab=bulk-data">Federal Election Commission</a>.
      Each member's page shows how much they raised from individuals in total, but individual donors are only
      named when they give large sums to outside spenders (below).
    </p>
    <p>
      A PAC's money comes from the employees, members or owners of the organization that sponsors it,
      not from the organization's treasury. Corporations can't give directly to federal candidates.
    </p>

    <h2>Outside spending and big donors</h2>
    <p>
      Super PACs and other groups can raise and spend unlimited amounts for or against candidates, as long as they
      don't coordinate with the campaign. These <em>independent expenditures</em> are shown separately from
      contributions, split into spending supporting and opposing each member, because money spent attacking
      someone isn't money given to them.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every group that spent on current members (other than party committees and campaigns), donations of
      $50,000 or more are listed as reported to the FEC. A donor's money isn't traced to particular ads: once given, it's pooled with
      everyone else's.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is behind each PAC</h2>
    <p>Each PAC is traced to the organization behind it, in this order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>FEC filings</strong> name a PAC's connected organization and its type (corporation, labor union, trade association, membership group), and the members of Congress who run leadership PACs.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.sec.gov/edgar">SEC EDGAR</a></strong> gives an industry code for publicly traded companies.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://lda.gov/">Lobbying disclosures</a></strong> list the issues an organization lobbies on, and which PACs belong to which lobbying registrants.</li>
      <li>Keywords in the organization's name (“Association of Realtors”, “Physicians”) for associations with no SEC or lobbying record.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      These feed a fixed set of sectors. PACs with no sponsoring organization are counted as{" "}
      <em>Ideological &amp; Single-Issue</em>. Some classifications are set by hand; the rules are
      in the project's source code. Mistakes are possible, so check the linked FEC filings for anything important.
    </p>

    <h2 id="labeler">The Bluesky labeler</h2>
    <p>
      Members of Congress with a Bluesky account are labeled with their top funding sectors and organizations
      by the <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/contributions.fyi">@contributions.fyi</a> labeler.
      Subscribe to it to see the labels on their posts.{" "}
      <a href="/bluesky/">How the labels work, and every feed, list and starter pack we publish</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="muted small">Data last updated {generatedAt.toLocaleDateString("en-US", { dateStyle: "long" })}.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 680px; font-size: var(--fs-md); line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: var(--sp-7); padding-top: var(--sp-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .prose p, .prose ol { margin: 0 0 var(--sp-4); }
  .prose li { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
</style>
